<div class="path-head"><p class="featured-region">Reading path · 10 stops</p><h1 class="path-title">Midwinter Festivals</h1><p class="path-tagline">How the darkest days of the year have been marked from Rome to Japan.</p><p class="path-intro">The winter solstice gives the Northern Hemisphere its shortest day and longest night on 21 or 22 December, and festivals have marked it since prehistory as the point when daylight begins to increase again. These stops follow that season across Eurasia: the Roman Saturnalia, the Yule of the heathen Germanic peoples, which later merged with Christmas, and the Slavic koliada, some of whose rituals date to pre-Christian times. They then turn to Yalda Night in Iran, the bonfires of Lohri in Punjab and Dongzhi in China, and end with Ōmisoka, the last day of the Japanese year.</p><p class="path-types">Suits <a href="/start">the Thinker</a> · <a href="/start">the Timekeeper</a></p><p class="path-print-row"><button type="button" class="ws-print path-print">Print this path</button></p></div>
<ol class="path-steps"><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/winter-solstice"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/winter-solstice.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">1 · Encyclopedia · Idea</span><span class="path-step-name">Winter solstice</span><span class="path-step-why">The shortest day and longest night of the year has been marked since prehistory as the point when daylight begins to increase; Newgrange and Stonehenge are aligned with it.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The winter solstice is the moment when one of Earth's poles reaches its maximum tilt away from the Sun, giving that hemisphere its shortest day and longest night of the year.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/saturnalia"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saturnalia.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">2 · Encyclopedia · Idea</span><span class="path-step-name">Saturnalia</span><span class="path-step-why">Rome's festival of Saturn ran for seven days to 23 December, with a public banquet, gift-giving, gambling and masters serving their slaves at table.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Saturnalia was an ancient Roman festival in honour of the god Saturn, held on 17 December in the Julian calendar and extended by the 1st century BCE to 23 December, seven days in all.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/religion/norse-religion"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oseberg-ship.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">3 · Profile · c. 500 CE – c. 1100 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Norse religion</span><span class="path-step-why">The pre-Christian religion of Scandinavia worshipped Odin, Thor, Freyr and Freyja with sacrifices and feasts led by chieftains and kings.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/yule"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yule.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">4 · Encyclopedia · Idea</span><span class="path-step-name">Yule</span><span class="path-step-why">Feasting, ceremonial drinking and oath swearing were central to this Germanic winter festival, which merged with Christmas and still names it in Northern European languages.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Yule is a winter festival and time of the year observed by heathen Germanic peoples and later merged with Christmas during Christianisation.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/religion/slavic-paganism"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/svantevit.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">5 · Profile · c. 500 CE – c. 1331 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Slavic paganism</span><span class="path-step-why">Slavic rulers adopted Christianity between 863 and 988, but popular resistance lasted for centuries and the old rites lived on in a “double faith”.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/koliada"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/koliada.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">6 · Encyclopedia · Idea</span><span class="path-step-name">Koliada</span><span class="path-step-why">The Slavic name for the days from Christmas to Epiphany covers rituals of which some date to pre-Christian times, among them carols sung from house to house for gifts.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Koliada, or koleda, is the traditional Slavic name for the period from Christmas to Epiphany or, more generally, for Christmas-related rituals, some of which date to pre-Christian times.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/yalda-night"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yalda-night.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">7 · Encyclopedia · Idea</span><span class="path-step-name">Yalda Night</span><span class="path-step-why">On the longest night of the year families in Iran and its neighbours gather to eat, drink and read Hafez; in Zoroastrian tradition the night was inauspicious.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Yalda Night, also called Chilla Night, is an ancient festival celebrated on the winter solstice of the Northern Hemisphere in Iran, Kurdistan, Uzbekistan, Tajikistan, Afghanistan, Azerbaijan and Turkmenistan.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/lohri"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lohri.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">8 · Encyclopedia · Idea</span><span class="path-step-name">Lohri</span><span class="path-step-why">Punjab marks the passing of the winter solstice with bonfires, festive foods, bhangra and songs of Dulla Bhatti on the night before Makar Sankranti.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Lohri is a midwinter folk and harvest festival of the Punjab region of India and Pakistan and other parts of northern India, marking the passing of the winter solstice and the end of winter.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/dongzhi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dongzhi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">9 · Encyclopedia · Idea</span><span class="path-step-name">Dongzhi</span><span class="path-step-why">China's winter solstice festival, first celebrated under the Zhou and made official under the Han, reads the lengthening days through yin and yang.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Dongzhi is the 22nd solar term of the Chinese calendar, marking the winter solstice, and the festival that Chinese people observe at that time, around 21 to 23 December.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/omisoka"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/omisoka.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">10 · Encyclopedia · Idea</span><span class="path-step-name">Ōmisoka</span><span class="path-step-why">Japan closes the year with house cleaning, repaid debts and long noodles, and at midnight most Buddhist temples strike a large bell 108 times.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Ōmisoka is the traditional Japanese celebration of the last day of the year.</span></span></a></li></ol>
